---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import icon from '../assets/icon.png';
---

<header class="site-header">
  <a class="brand" href="/">
    <Image src={icon} alt="" width={32} height={32} />
    <span>Sealshot</span>
  </a>
  <nav aria-label="Main">
    <!-- No Features link. It was the only item that was not a page: from /buy/,
         /support/ or /download/ it navigated home AND jumped past the hero, so a
         visitor skipped the headline and the price line to reach a grid they were
         already scrolling toward. The #features id stays, for the footer link and
         any external one. (Removing it did NOT by itself unwrap the nav on a
         phone — the wrap is fixed in global.css by tightening the mobile column
         gap. That fix was measured with the wider "Pricing" label, so "Buy"
         only gives width back.) -->
    <a href="/docs/">Docs</a>
    <!-- Guides next to Docs: both are reading, and the pair reads as one shelf.
         Docs answer "how does this feature work"; Guides answer "how do I solve
         this problem", which is how people arrive from a search engine. Before
         FAQ, because FAQ belongs beside Support — see below. -->
    <a href="/guides/">Guides</a>
    <!-- FAQ sits between Docs and Support because it is where most support
         questions are already answered: reaching it before Support saves the
         round trip. It points into the docs (/docs/faq/), which is where the
         page lives — same target as the footer link. -->
    <a href="/docs/faq/">FAQ</a>
    <a href="/support/">Support</a>
    <!-- Community after Support: both are "talk to a person", and this one is
         the public half of that pair — issues, releases, the repo. It ranks
         below Support because someone with a problem should reach the inbox
         that answers them first, not a tracker.

         The seventh item is what pushed the header past the width of a tablet:
         every label is one word, so nothing shrinks, and the nav's min-content
         went from 525px to 640px. The fluid gap in global.css is what absorbs
         that between 704px and 838px. -->
    <a href="/community/">Community</a>
    <!-- The two pills are wrapped so they wrap together. `display: contents` on
         a wide screen means this span is not there at all — the flex row is
         byte-identical to what it was. Below 640px it becomes a flex group, so
         the sixth nav item pushes BOTH actions onto the second line instead of
         orphaning Download on a line of its own, which is the exact failure the
         mobile rules in global.css were written to fix. -->
    <span class="nav-actions">
      <!-- Donate, because that is what the page now is. Not "Support" — that
           word is taken by the help page two links to the left, and one word
           meaning two destinations in the same nav is a coin-flip. Outlined while
           Download is filled, so the free download stays the primary entry point. -->
      <a class="cta outline" href="/donate/">Donate</a>
      <a class="cta" href="/download/">Download</a>
    </span>
  </nav>
</header>
